# Commit conventions — MyConciergeHotel.com

We follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) with project-specific scopes.

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <short summary>

<optional body>

<optional footer(s)>
```

## Types
- `feat`: new feature
- `fix`: bug fix
- `chore`: tooling, scaffolding, dependency bumps
- `refactor`: refactor without behavior change
- `perf`: performance improvement
- `docs`: documentation only
- `test`: tests only
- `style`: formatting, no logic
- `revert`: revert previous commit

## Scopes (project-specific)
- `repo` — root tooling, CI, monorepo plumbing
- `web` — `apps/web`
- `admin` — `apps/admin`
- `db` — `packages/db`
- `domain` — `packages/domain`
- `integrations` — generic integrations changes
- `amadeus`, `little`, `makcorps`, `apify`, `gplaces`, `brevo`, `algolia`, `redis`, `sentry` — specific integration
- `seo`, `geo`, `aeo`, `jsonld`, `sitemap`, `robots`, `llms`
- `booking`, `search`, `loyalty`, `pricing`, `editorial`, `account`, `auth`, `email`
- `ui` — shared UI
- `docs` — documentation
- `skills` — `.cursor/skills/`

## Examples

```
feat(booking): persist cancellation policy verbatim in bookings table

fix(amadeus): refresh OAuth token on 401 with mutex to avoid stampede

docs(seo): document anti-cannibalisation 301 matrix

chore(repo): bump turbo to 2.5.0 and pnpm to 10.33.2

feat(skills): bootstrap 29 cursor skills covering CDC v3.0 §14

refactor(domain): extract Booking state machine into dedicated module
```

## Rules
- Subject in imperative mood ("add", "fix", not "added", "fixes").
- ≤ 72 chars subject line; body wraps at 100 chars.
- Reference ADRs in body when relevant: `Refs: docs/adr/0003-payload-cms.md`.
- Breaking changes flagged with `!` and `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.

## Concurrent / multi-agent commits — pathspec, never `git add -A`

When several agents (background subagents in multitask mode) share **one
working tree**, they share **one Git index**. A `git add -A` / `git add .`
from one agent stages the whole tree; a peer's `git commit` then sweeps those
files into the wrong commit (cross-contamination), and simultaneous staging
collides on `.git/index.lock`.

**Always commit with an explicit pathspec** — it commits ONLY the named paths
and ignores the rest of the shared index:

```powershell
# ✅ scoped to these files only
git commit path/to/file-a.ts path/to/file-b.md -m "feat(scope): ..."

# ❌ in a shared tree — stages everything, a peer commit can grab it
git add -A
git commit -m "feat(scope): ..."
```

Retry ~3s on a transient `index.lock` held by a peer's in-flight commit; never
delete a lock you didn't create. Full operational recipe (PowerShell retry
loop, caveats) in the `windows-dev-environment` skill §Rule 9 quinquies
(`.cursor/skills/windows-dev-environment/SKILL.md`).

## `Tested:` footer (mandatory for user-visible changes)

When the commit modifies a user-visible surface (route, UI block, copy,
nav, form, JSON-LD), append a `Tested:` footer that summarises the
walk-through evidence (URLs walked, locales, discoverability path,
viewports). This proves the agent followed the hard rule
`user-acceptance-before-commit` rather than relying on green CI alone.

```
feat(club): add Le Concierge Club ribbon on the homepage

Tested: walked /fr/, /en/ via cursor-ide-browser MCP. Ribbon visible
  above-the-fold on desktop (1280) + mobile (375). Discoverable via
  header mega-menu (1 click) and 2 ribbon CTAs (0 clicks). Score 4/4.
```

See `.cursor/skills/user-acceptance-loop/SKILL.md` for the recipe.
